          <p>
            Read that table before you buy anything. If your question is about hardness for a softener, chlorine taste or whether a filter cartridge is spent, strips are the right and cheapest tool. If your question is whether your water is safe for a baby, a pregnant household member or anyone drinking it every day for years, the answer comes from a lab, and the strip kit is at best a reason to order one.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good home testing routine often uses more than one type: a lab panel once to establish a baseline, a targeted lab test for lead if the house is old, and strips or a TDS meter for routine checks on the equipment you install as a result. That combination costs less over five years than repeatedly buying cheap kits that never answer the question.
          </p>
