        <div id="strips-vs-lab" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Test Strips vs Reagent Kits vs Mail-In Lab Tests
          </h2>

          <p>
            Test strips win on speed, cost per test and convenience. A 100-strip kit costs about the same as a takeaway lunch, gives results in a couple of minutes and lets you test every tap in the house, before and after a filter, as often as you like. The weaknesses are colour judgement, which varies with lighting and eyesight, and the coarse steps on the colour chart. Two people reading the same strip in different rooms can easily land one step apart on the chart.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reagent kits like the Watersafe WS-425W are a step up for specific tests. Each parameter has its own packet or vial and instructions, and the bacteria test is a real culture: you add water to a growth medium, wait 48 hours and read a colour change. They are single-use and more fiddly than strips, but they are designed around regulatory limits, so the lead test is built to show whether dissolved lead is above or below the EPA's 15 ppb action level rather than estimating an exact number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mail-in lab tests give you numbers with detection limits. You fill bottles following the instructions, which usually specify a first-draw sample for lead, ship them back in the prepaid box and get a report in about a week or two. The cost is higher, from tens of dollars for a lead test to several hundred for a 200-parameter panel, and the sampling instructions matter: a sample that sat too long in a warm truck, or was taken after running the tap for five minutes, gives a misleading answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which one to buy first</h3>

          <p>
            If you have never tested your water, buy a lab test first, not strips. One good lab result tells you which parameters to keep an eye on, and then strips or a meter become useful for tracking those specific numbers. Buying strips first usually ends with a vague result on the lead pad, a worried household and a lab test anyway, so you pay twice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The exception is a pure plumbing question. If you only want to know how hard your water is so you can set a softener's regeneration, a strip kit gives you the grains-per-gallon figure you need in two minutes, and a lab test would be a waste of money. Our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">water softener guide</a> explains how to turn that hardness figure into a softener size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Testing for Lead: Why Strips Are Not Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lead gets into drinking water almost entirely from plumbing, not from the source. Lead service lines, lead-based solder on copper pipe joints and older brass fixtures release it as water sits in the pipes. That has two consequences for testing. First, the level varies from tap to tap and from hour to hour. Second, the sample that matters is the first water out of the tap after it has sat unused for at least six hours, which is how labs and utilities sample for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The EPA's long-standing action level for lead in public water systems is 15 parts per billion, and its 2024 Lead and Copper Rule Improvements lower that to 10 ppb for utilities over the next few years. The EPA's health goal for lead is zero, because there is no known safe level for children. That is the problem with strip testing: the decisions you care about sit between 0 and 15 ppb, and a colour pad cannot place a result inside that range with any confidence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the strip's lead pad, if it has one, as a smoke alarm. A clear positive on a strip is a reason to stop drinking from that tap and order a lab test immediately. A negative strip result is not reassurance. The Tap Score Lead & Copper kit or a state-certified local lab is the right test for any house built before 1986, when the federal ban on lead pipe and solder took effect, and for any home with a known lead service line.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sample lead first thing in the morning, before anyone runs the tap.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Lead builds up as water sits in the pipes overnight. A sample taken after showers and coffee will read lower than what you actually drink first thing, which is the number the lab is trying to catch.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the lab result is elevated, the fixes in order of cost are: run the cold tap for a minute or two before drinking until you have a long-term fix, use only cold water for drinking and cooking, fit a filter certified to NSF/ANSI Standard 53 for lead reduction at the kitchen tap, and contact your utility about service line replacement. Our guides to the <a href="/best-faucet-water-filter/">best faucet water filters</a> and <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">under-sink water filters</a> both flag which models carry lead certification.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="well-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Well Water Testing: What and How Often
          </h2>

          <p>
            Private wells are not covered by the federal Safe Drinking Water Act. Nobody tests your well for you, and nobody tells you when it changes. The CDC recommends that well owners test at least once a year for total coliform bacteria, nitrates, total dissolved solids and pH, and more often after flooding, after work on the well or pump, or when the water's taste, smell or colour changes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bacteria is the first priority because it is the most immediate health risk. A coliform test is a presence or absence test: coliforms themselves are usually harmless, but their presence means surface water or soil is getting into the well, and that path can carry pathogens such as E. coli. A positive result means confirm it with a certified lab, then shock-chlorinate the well and retest, and look for the cause: a cracked cap, poor grading around the casing or a nearby septic problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nitrate is the second priority, especially in farm country. The EPA limit is 10 mg/L as nitrogen, and high levels are a specific risk for infants fed formula made with well water. Nitrate comes from fertiliser, manure and septic systems, it rises after heavy spring rain, and it is not removed by boiling. Strip and reagent kits read nitrate reasonably well near the 10 ppm limit, which makes them a fair screen between lab tests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond those, test once for arsenic, uranium, manganese and radon in water if your region is known for them, which your state geological survey or health department will tell you. These are geology problems, they do not usually change year to year, and only a lab can measure them at the levels that matter. A rotten-egg smell points to hydrogen sulphide or sulphate-reducing bacteria; our guide on <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/">why water smells like rotten eggs</a> covers that diagnosis.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical schedule for a well: a broad lab panel like the Tap Score Advanced when you buy the house or drill the well, then a coliform and nitrate test every year, either through your county health department, which often offers well tests at low cost, or with a reagent kit like the Watersafe WS-425W, and a fresh lab test after any flood or major repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="city-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. City Water: Read the Consumer Confidence Report First
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are on municipal water, you already have free test results. Every community water system in the US must publish an annual Consumer Confidence Report, usually by July 1, listing the regulated contaminants it detected, the levels found and how those compare with federal limits. Search your utility's name plus "consumer confidence report" or "water quality report" and you will usually find it as a PDF.
          </p>

          <p>
            The CCR tells you about water as it leaves the treatment plant and at sampled points in the distribution system. It cannot tell you about the pipes between the street and your tap, which is exactly where lead and copper come from. It also reports averages and ranges across the system, so a single high reading in your neighbourhood can be hidden in a range.
          </p>

          <p>
            So for city water, a sensible test plan is narrow: read the CCR, then test your own tap for lead and copper if the house is older, and use strips for chlorine and hardness if you are choosing a filter for taste or scale. A 200-parameter panel on treated city water mostly confirms what the utility already measured. If your CCR shows a contaminant near its limit, or reports a violation, a targeted lab test for that specific contaminant is worth more than a broad one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chlorine and chloramine are the most common taste complaint on city water, and a strip kit shows you how much free chlorine is there. Chloramine is more stable and harder to remove than chlorine, so it is worth knowing which your utility uses before buying a carbon filter. The CCR usually says which disinfectant is used, and the <a href="/best-countertop-water-filter/">best countertop water filters</a> and <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitchers</a> differ a lot in how well they handle it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pfas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. PFAS and the Other Things Strips Cannot See
          </h2>

          <p>
            PFAS, the so-called forever chemicals, are the clearest example of a contaminant no home kit can detect. The EPA set enforceable limits of 4 parts per trillion for PFOA and PFOS in 2024. A part per trillion is roughly one drop in twenty Olympic swimming pools. Detecting that requires liquid chromatography with mass spectrometry in a laboratory using an EPA method such as 533 or 537.1, and careful sampling, because PFAS are in many household products and can contaminate a sample.
          </p>

          <p>
            Any strip or home kit that claims to detect PFAS at health-relevant levels should be treated with suspicion. Tap Score, Safe Home and other lab services sell dedicated PFAS tests, and they are among the most expensive tests in their catalogues because the analysis is expensive. Check whether your utility already tests for PFAS, since many larger systems now publish those results, before paying for one yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to volatile organic compounds such as benzene and solvents, to most pesticides and herbicides, and to radiological contaminants such as uranium and radium. These are lab-only tests. They are worth paying for when there is a reason to suspect them: a well near a petrol station, a landfill, an industrial site, intensive agriculture or a known regional problem. They are not worth paying for out of general anxiety on treated city water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that a cheap kit coming back clean on every pad tells you nothing about any of these contaminants. That is not a flaw in the kit; it is outside what colour chemistry can do. Buy the cheap kit for what it can measure, and know what it cannot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What a TDS Meter Tells You, and What It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Total dissolved solids meters are cheap, quick and widely misused. A TDS meter measures how well water conducts electricity and converts that into an estimate of dissolved mineral content in parts per million. Calcium, magnesium, sodium, bicarbonate and chloride make up most of that number in typical tap water. The EPA's secondary, non-enforceable guideline for TDS is 500 mg/L, which is about taste and scale, not health.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where a meter earns its place is reverse osmosis maintenance. An RO membrane removes most dissolved solids, so comparing tap water with RO water gives you a quick reading on whether the membrane is still doing its job. Measure both when the system is new, write the numbers down, and test every month or two. When the percentage removed drops noticeably, the membrane or pre-filters are due. Our <a href="/best-reverse-osmosis-water-filter-pitcher/">reverse osmosis pitcher guide</a> uses the same approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it fails is safety. Lead at 50 ppb, which is more than three times the EPA action level, is 0.05 ppm and does not move a TDS reading meaningfully. Bacteria, PFAS, pesticides and most organics barely register. Meanwhile, perfectly safe hard water from limestone can read 400 ppm. A high reading does not mean danger, and a low one does not mean safety.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be particularly wary of in-home sales demonstrations that use a TDS meter, or an electrolysis device that turns water brown, to show that your water is contaminated. Both effects come mainly from harmless minerals and the device itself. If someone is selling you a treatment system based on a TDS reading, ask for a certified lab result instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certified-lab" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Pay for a Certified Lab
          </h2>

          <p>
            A certified lab test is worth the money in six situations: you are buying a house with a private well, there is a baby or pregnant person in the household, the house was built before 1986 and you have never tested for lead, your well has flooded or had major work, a strip or reagent kit returned a positive for bacteria, nitrate or lead, or you are about to spend serious money on a whole-house treatment system and need to size it correctly.
          </p>

          <p>
            You have two routes. Consumer services such as Tap Score and Safe Home sell kits on Amazon, handle the shipping and translate the results into plain language with treatment advice. Your state or county health department can point you to state-certified drinking water labs, which are sometimes cheaper, sometimes free for basic well tests, and are often what a mortgage lender or local regulation requires. Before choosing a consumer kit, confirm the lab it uses is certified for the specific analytes you need.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Test to buy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">City water, old house</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lead & copper lab test</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lead comes from your pipes, not the plant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
