                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strips</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chlorine and hardness only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Private well, first test</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Broad lab panel plus bacteria</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Establishes your baseline</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Private well, annual</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coliform and nitrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">CDC minimum for wells</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Near industry or airport</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">PFAS lab test</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only a lab can see ppt levels</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">RO system maintenance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">TDS meter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tracks membrane performance</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sampling technique decides whether a lab result is useful. Follow the kit's instructions on the letter: which tap, whether to run it first, whether to remove the aerator, how to keep the sample cool and when to ship. Most kits ask you to sample early in the week so the bottles do not sit in a depot over a weekend. A rushed or wrong sample wastes the most expensive part of the process.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, test the water you actually drink. If you already have a filter, test the unfiltered tap to understand the source and the filtered tap to confirm the filter works. Testing only the filtered water hides the problem the day the cartridge is exhausted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-results" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What to Do With Your Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            The point of testing is to buy the right treatment, or none. Match each finding to a fix: lead calls for a filter certified to NSF/ANSI 53 for lead at the drinking tap; hardness calls for a softener or a scale-reduction system; chlorine taste calls for activated carbon; nitrate and high TDS call for reverse osmosis; coliform bacteria calls for fixing the well and possibly UV disinfection. Our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">water filtration system guide</a> covers the whole-house options in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the certification, not the marketing. A filter that claims to remove lead should list NSF/ANSI 53 for lead reduction, and one that claims to reduce PFAS should list NSF/ANSI 53 or 58 for PFOA and PFOS. Many filters reduce chlorine taste and nothing else, which is fine if that is your only problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then retest. Test again after installing any treatment to prove it works on your water, and set a schedule: strips or a TDS meter monthly or quarterly for equipment checks, an annual bacteria and nitrate test for a well, and a lead retest after any plumbing work. Keep the lab reports; they are useful when selling the house and when comparing results over the years.
          </p>

          <p>
            If nothing was found, that is a result too. Plenty of households test, find their water is fine, and save the cost of a filter they did not need. That is the best outcome a water test can give you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Negative Lead Strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Strip lead pads cannot reliably separate 2 ppb from 12 ppb, and the decisions that matter sit in that range. Treat a positive as a reason to order a lab test and a negative as no information. For any house built before 1986, a lab lead test is the only answer that counts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sampling Lead After Running the Tap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lead accumulates while water sits in household plumbing, so a sample taken mid-afternoon after the tap has run all day understates what you drink first thing in the morning. Take a first-draw sample after at least six hours of no use, exactly as the kit instructions specify.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a TDS Meter as a Safety Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                TDS meters measure dissolved minerals. Lead, bacteria, PFAS and pesticides barely change the reading, and safe hard water can read high. Use a meter to check a reverse osmosis membrane, never to decide whether water is safe to drink or to justify buying a system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Annual Well Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nobody regulates a private well, and nitrate and bacteria can change after heavy rain, flooding or nearby construction. The CDC recommends testing at least once a year for coliform bacteria, nitrates, TDS and pH, plus after any flood or repair. A clean result five years ago is not a clean result today.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a 200-Parameter Panel on City Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Your utility already monitors regulated contaminants and publishes the results in its Consumer Confidence Report. On treated city water, a targeted lead and copper test of your own tap usually tells you more for far less money. Save the broad panels for wells and special risks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

