        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most accurate home water test kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A mail-in lab test is the most accurate option, because a laboratory measures contaminants with instruments and reports numbers with detection limits. The Tap Score Advanced panel and Safe Home ULTIMATE both send samples to EPA-certified labs, per their listings. Strips and reagent kits are screening tools: good for hardness, chlorine and pH, weak for lead at low levels, and blind to PFAS.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a water test kit detect lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can, with limits. Strips with a lead pad give a rough screen, and the Watersafe WS-425W is designed to show whether dissolved lead is above the EPA's 15 ppb action level. Neither can measure low single-digit ppb levels reliably. For an actual lead number, use a lab test such as Tap Score's Lead & Copper kit with a first-draw sample.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I test well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The CDC recommends at least once a year for total coliform bacteria, nitrates, total dissolved solids and pH. Test again after flooding, after work on the well or pump, if anyone in the house gets a stomach illness that might be waterborne, or when taste, smell or colour changes. Test once for arsenic, uranium and other local geology-driven contaminants.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do water test strips detect bacteria?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard strips do not. Bacteria testing needs a culture, where water sits in a growth medium for 24 to 48 hours and changes colour if coliforms are present. Kits like the Varify 17-in-1 and Watersafe WS-425W include separate bacteria vials for this. A positive at-home bacteria result should be confirmed by a certified lab before you disinfect.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I test for PFAS at home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not with a strip or reagent kit. PFAS limits are measured in parts per trillion, which requires laboratory mass spectrometry. Lab services such as Tap Score and Safe Home sell dedicated PFAS kits that you sample at home and mail in. Check your utility's water quality report first, because many larger systems now publish PFAS results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a TDS meter a good water test?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for one job. A TDS meter estimates dissolved minerals and is excellent for confirming a reverse osmosis membrane is still working. It cannot detect lead, bacteria, PFAS or pesticides, and a high reading often just means hard, mineral-rich water. Never buy a treatment system on the strength of a TDS reading alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I test city water if my utility already tests it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Read your utility's Consumer Confidence Report first. It covers the water leaving the plant and in the mains, not your house plumbing. If your home was built before 1986 or has a lead service line, test your own tap for lead and copper. Otherwise, strips for chlorine and hardness are usually enough to choose a filter.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

